questions.question:
The ratio \(\frac{K_P}{K_C}\) for the reaction :
CO_{(g)} + \frac{1}{2}O_{2(g)} \rightleftharpoons CO_{2(g)}
is :

questions.explanation:
The given reaction is:
CO_{(g)} + \frac{1}{2}O_{2(g)} \rightleftharpoons CO_{2(g)}
First, calculate the change in the number of moles of gaseous products and reactants (\(\Delta n_g\)): 
\Delta n_g = \text{moles of gaseous products} - \text{moles of gaseous reactants}
\Delta n_g = 1 - (1 + \frac{1}{2}) = 1 - \frac{3}{2} = -\frac{1}{2}
The relationship between \(K_P\) and \(K_C\) is given by:
K_P = K_C (RT)^{\Delta n_g}
Substitute the value of \(\Delta n_g\):
K_P = K_C (RT)^{-1/2}
To find the ratio \(\frac{K_P}{K_C}\), rearrange the equation:
\frac{K_P}{K_C} = (RT)^{-1/2}
This can also be written as:
\frac{K_P}{K_C} = \frac{1}{(RT)^{1/2}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{RT}}

